"""Original participatory ecological assessment teaching."""
def course(c,m):
    return c("SWP-043","Participatory Ecological Vulnerability Assessment","participatory-ecological-vulnerability-assessment","Participatory Ecological\nVulnerability Assessment",
        "Prepare an ethical participatory assessment protocol and a provisional risk register that preserves local knowledge, uncertainty and technical scope.",[
m("Purpose participation and safe information gathering","Design a bounded assessment process with clear influence, suitable participation and proportionate information handling.",[
"An assessment should answer a defined decision question. Establish what the information will help decide, who has authority over that decision and what cannot be promised. An introductory community assessment might identify barriers to reaching a service during heavy rain; it does not certify a site's physical safety or replace an environmental impact assessment. Explain these boundaries before inviting people to contribute. If the purpose changes, revisit the information required and the arrangements under which it can be used rather than treating the original invitation as unlimited permission. [S1, S3]",
"Plan participation before choosing the method. Identify groups likely to have relevant experiences and ask what could prevent them from contributing. Timing, transport, communication needs, caring duties and fear of consequences may matter. Offer an appropriate alternative to public speaking where feasible. Explain which decisions remain open and how contributions will be considered. The participation review highlights power and exclusion, but its English-language Scopus coverage ends in 2020. It informs process questions rather than proving that a method will be inclusive in this community. [R2]",
"Match information collection to the actual question. A discussion about a public route may not require names, health histories or precise household locations. Where personal data are necessary, use the organisation's approved basis, consent arrangements where applicable, access controls and retention process. Do not post an identifiable map simply because participants helped draw it. Discuss whether an entry could expose someone to stigma, conflict or other harm. Provide a way to correct an account and explain any relevant confidentiality limits through the authorised process. [S1]",
"A method can include a facilitated discussion, an accessible mapping exercise, a seasonal calendar or an agreed observation, depending on purpose and local suitability. None should require learners or residents to enter hazardous places or collect technical samples outside their training. Plan how uncertainty, disagreement and missing perspectives will be recorded. The education benchmark links engagement and assessment to informed practice, but selecting a tool from a learning course is not formal approval for field research. Seek the relevant service, ethics or safeguarding review before an actual exercise requires it. [S3, S1]"],
"Fictional case: an assessment asks how rain affects access to a learning centre. Some participants cannot attend the public meeting. The team offers a suitable alternative, explains which route decisions remain open and avoids collecting household health details that are unnecessary for the access question.",
"Write a one-page protocol: decision question, authority, participation arrangements, method, necessary information, confidentiality limits and review requirements. Include an alternative for someone unable to attend.",
"A strong protocol states both purpose and limits, gives people a meaningful route to contribute and minimises unnecessary information. It does not present an introductory assessment as technical certification.",["S1","S3","R2"],[
("An assessment begins with a defined decision question.","A team wants to collect community information but has not identified its use. What should be clarified first?","The decision question, authority and limits of the exercise.",["How to collect the largest possible dataset.","Which personal details would make the report more striking.","How to promise every requested project before assessment."],"Purpose determines which information and process are justified."),
("Participation arrangements need alternatives to a single meeting.","People with caring responsibilities cannot attend the main session. What should the protocol consider?","A suitable alternative way to contribute and clear decision boundaries.",["Treating absence as agreement with the meeting.","Asking attendees to invent the absent group's views.","Closing contributions because one session was held."],"Attendance barriers should inform engagement rather than define whose views count."),
("Collect only information justified by the assessment purpose.","A public-route discussion does not need health histories. What is the appropriate collection choice?","Leave unnecessary health details out of the assessment.",["Collect every history in case it becomes useful later.","Publish the details because the topic concerns the community.","Treat names as essential for any map."],"Data minimisation follows the defined purpose."),
("A participatory map may still reveal sensitive information.","Residents mark household circumstances on a draft map. What must be checked before sharing?","Purpose, authority and whether the map could identify or harm people.",["Only whether the map looks attractive.","Whether participation automatically permits unrestricted publication.","Whether every personal location can be treated as public."],"Co-creation does not remove confidentiality and protection questions."),
("An introductory method does not authorise hazardous field activity.","A learner proposes entering a flooded area to improve a map. What should the plan do?","Use safe authorised methods and seek the appropriate technical assessment.",["Proceed because community interest overrides safety arrangements.","Treat the course as an environmental sampling qualification.","Ask untrained residents to enter instead."],"Assessment purpose does not justify unsafe or unqualified fieldwork."),
]),
m("Risk registers and triangulation","Separate hazard, exposure, vulnerability and capacity while recording the source and limits of each entry.",[
"A provisional risk register organises a concern so that it can be discussed and checked. Distinguish a potential harmful event or condition, the people or assets that may be exposed, the circumstances affecting susceptibility and the resources available to respond. These categories overlap in real life but answer different questions. A rain-related route problem is not fully described by naming heavy rain. Consider who uses the route, when use occurs, which barriers matter and what alternative arrangements already exist. Avoid converting the categories into a precise prediction without an appropriate method. [S2]",
"Record the source, date, location or scale, and information status for each relevant entry. A resident's report, a direct observation, a service record and a technical projection are different forms of evidence. Each may be useful, but they should not be presented as interchangeable. Check whether a map or record is current and whether its geographic scale fits the decision. A city-level estimate may guide questions while failing to describe a particular street or household. Note any missing information and identify who could appropriately verify it. [S2, S3]",
"Triangulation compares sources to understand agreement, difference and gaps. It does not mean selecting the account that supports the preferred project. A service directory may list a usable route while residents describe a barrier at a particular time. Check dates, definitions and whose experience each source covers. Do not average away a meaningful disagreement. The IJSDS waterfront study illustrates interacting spatial, economic and environmental conditions in three Port Harcourt communities. Its descriptive findings can inform questions, while an assessment must still examine the actual local circumstances. [R8]",
"Include capacity and uncertainty alongside concern. A trusted contact, local warning practice or available transport may support a response without eliminating the underlying risk. Distinguish an existing arrangement from a proposed resource that has no confirmed owner or funding. If a concern requires urgent action, use the relevant response process rather than wait for a complete register. If technical evidence is unavailable, record the gap explicitly and seek appropriate advice. An honest provisional entry can support a safer decision than a polished score with no defensible basis. [S1, S2]"],
"Fictional case: residents report that a path is difficult after rain, while a municipal map lists it as an access route. The register records the map date, reported timing and affected journeys, plus an existing alternative. A qualified service is asked to assess the physical route; no learner enters the hazardous area.",
"Create a provisional register with columns for concern, exposure, vulnerability, capacity, source/date, uncertainty and verification owner. Complete three fictional entries and mark an unsupported assumption.",
"A useful register separates categories and evidence status. It keeps disagreement and capacities visible and gives a route to verify assumptions rather than inventing a precise risk probability.",["S2","S3","R8","S1"],[
("Hazard alone does not describe the full risk context.","A register names heavy rain but nothing about route users or access barriers. What is missing?","Relevant exposure, vulnerability and capacity information.",["A more dramatic label for the same hazard.","A guaranteed outcome for every household.","A statement that all residents face identical consequences."],"The physical condition is only one part of the decision context."),
("Evidence scale must fit the proposed decision.","A city-wide estimate is used as an exact figure for one street. What needs checking?","Whether its scale and method support that local use.",["Whether a precise-looking number can be accepted without context.","Whether the street can be assumed to match every city average.","Whether the source date can be omitted."],"A valid estimate at one scale does not automatically describe another."),
("Triangulation investigates differences rather than selecting a convenient account.","A current service record and residents' reports appear inconsistent. What should the assessment do?","Check timing, definitions and coverage while retaining the discrepancy.",["Delete the account that delays the preferred project.","Treat a majority vote as technical verification.","Average the statements into a fact neither source supports."],"Differences can identify a relevant condition or evidence gap."),
("Capacity can reduce a barrier without removing the underlying concern.","Neighbours provide occasional transport during disruption. How should it be recorded?","As a resource with its availability and limits stated.",["As proof that the route risk no longer exists.","As permanent funded capacity without confirmation.","As a reason to remove residents' reported difficulties."],"A helpful resource may still be limited or unreliable."),
("Uncertainty needs an explicit verification task.","A register contains an unconfirmed route restriction. What makes the entry reviewable?","A clear information status, gap and appropriate verification owner.",["A high score chosen to make action certain.","Removal of every uncertain concern from discussion.","A confirmed label because the report sounds plausible."],"Provisional information can be used honestly when its limits and next check are visible."),
]),
m("Validation prioritisation and feedback","Agree a transparent provisional priority and communicate how evidence and contributions affected it.",[
"Validation gives contributors an opportunity to check whether the assessment represents their account accurately. Explain the draft in an accessible form and distinguish a correction of fact from a difference in interpretation or priority. Ask whose perspective remains missing and whether sharing the draft creates a confidentiality concern. A validation meeting does not require everyone to agree. Record relevant disagreement and state how it will be carried into the decision. A claim that the community has validated the whole assessment should not be based on a small, unexamined attendance list. [R2, S1]",
"Agree prioritisation criteria before using them to rank concerns. Criteria might include the significance of an access barrier, the people affected, urgency, feasibility, uncertainty and the possibility of harm. State who agreed the criteria and who can authorise action. A simple qualitative comparison can be useful when its limits are clear. Avoid presenting an unvalidated numerical score as an objective probability or using a total to hide a serious protection concern. A technical risk decision may need a specialist method beyond this introductory assessment. [S2, S3]",
"Distinguish urgency from feasibility. A concern can be important even when the community team cannot resolve it alone. Identify an immediate authorised response where necessary and a separate route for longer-term assessment or resources. Check whether the preferred priority could disadvantage a group whose voice has been weak. The participation review supports examining who influences decisions, while the Port Harcourt study reminds learners that conditions can interact. Neither provides a ready-made ranking for a different neighbourhood or establishes that the highest workshop score will produce the greatest benefit. [R2, R8]",
"Close the feedback loop by explaining the decision, its basis, outstanding uncertainty and the next review point. Identify the responsible role and a suitable correction or complaint route. If people offered information but no action can yet be authorised, say so rather than describe an unfunded option as a commitment. Retain a proportionate assessment record with access limited through the approved process. Later evidence may require a revised priority. Treat this as accountable learning, preserving the earlier reasoning and explaining what changed, rather than silently replacing the original assessment. [S1, S3]"],
"Fictional case: a workshop ranks a convenient route change highly, but contributors later identify an access barrier affecting people absent from the session. The team revisits its criteria and records a provisional priority, the missing technical check and the role responsible for communicating the decision.",
"Write a priority note using three stated criteria. Include a disagreement, an evidence gap, an authorised next step and a feedback route. Explain what would cause the priority to be revisited.",
"Strong prioritisation is transparent about authority, criteria and uncertainty. It preserves concerns from missing groups and does not convert a workshop score into a technical probability or a funding promise.",["R2","S1","S2","S3","R8"],[
("Validation can preserve disagreement while correcting an account.","Contributors disagree about the most important barrier. What should the validation record show?","The relevant differences and how they enter the decision.",["An invented consensus to simplify the report.","Only the view of the person chairing the meeting.","A claim that disagreement invalidates every reported fact."],"Validation should improve the accuracy and transparency of the assessment."),
("Prioritisation criteria should be explicit before ranking.","A team changes its criteria after seeing which project wins. What is the concern?","The ranking may reflect a preferred outcome rather than a transparent comparison.",["Every ranking becomes more objective when criteria are hidden.","Criteria are unnecessary if a numerical total is available.","A chosen project retrospectively validates any scoring method."],"Transparent criteria help people examine how the decision was reached."),
("A workshop score is not an objective technical probability.","A qualitative exercise gives one concern the largest total. Which conclusion is justified?","It ranks highest under those stated criteria, with method limits retained.",["Its exact probability is now scientifically measured.","Every specialist assessment can be omitted.","Its intervention effect is proven by the total."],"The meaning of the score depends on the exercise used."),
("Importance and the team's ability to act are different questions.","A serious concern exceeds the team's resources. What should the priority note include?","Its significance and the appropriate authorised response or escalation route.",["A low importance label solely because the team cannot fund it.","A promise of resources controlled by another organisation.","A decision to hide the concern from contributors."],"Limited authority does not make an important concern disappear."),
("Feedback should distinguish a proposal from a commitment.","An option has no confirmed funding or decision owner. How should it be communicated?","As a proposal with the outstanding authority and resource questions stated.",["As a guaranteed project because participants support it.","As completed adaptation despite no implementation.","Without mentioning the unresolved decision."],"Honest feedback avoids creating an unsupported expectation."),
]),
])
